CS 214 Project III: A Simple Shell

Kevin Garcia - kag408
Joarder Rashid - jmr688

Build and run
-------------
Run make to compile mysh.c and run the tests. Each check prints PASS or
FAIL. The tests use sh and common Unix tools.

    make
    ./mysh
    ./mysh mysh_tests.txt
    printf 'echo hello\nexit\n' | ./mysh

make mysh builds without testing. make test repeats the tests.
make run starts the shell. make clean removes the executable.

How it works
------------
The shell uses read() to read a full line before executing it. The same
input loop and parser handle terminal input, piped input, and input files.
The parser separates arguments, comments, redirections, and pipes.
glob() expands * patterns. Other glob operators are escaped, and patterns
with no matches are passed through unchanged.

External commands use fork() and execv(). Bare names are searched in
/usr/local/bin, /usr/bin, and /bin, in that order. Built-ins are cd, pwd,
which, and exit. They run in the parent unless they are in a pipeline.
which looks for executable files even when given a built-in name.

Redirection uses open() and dup2(). New output files request mode 0640,
subject to umask. Standalone built-ins restore their original descriptors.
Pipeline stages start before the shell waits. Unused pipe ends are closed,
every child is waited for, and the last stage determines success.
Batch child input defaults to /dev/null unless a file or pipe supplies it.
An exit in a pipeline stops the shell after the whole job finishes.

Interactive mode prints the directory prompt, abbreviates HOME as ~,
and reports failed-command statuses. It also prints welcome and goodbye
messages. Batch mode does not print these messages or prompts.

Testing
-------
Run make test to use tests.sh. It creates temporary fixtures, compares
actual output with expected text, and checks exit statuses and file mode.
The temporary directory is removed afterward.

The 24 checks cover arguments, comments, explicit paths, final input
without a newline, exit arguments, batch child input, pwd, cd, HOME,
invalid built-in arguments, redirection, append, descriptor restoration,
file errors, multiple pipes, early-closing readers, pipeline cd, wildcards,
hidden files, unmatched patterns, syntax errors, unknown commands, which,
exit in pipelines, filename input, missing files, and output mode 0640.
All 24 passed in the development environment. Repeat them on iLab.
